{"maxId":258,"entries":{"chapter-33-study-low-vision-assessment-and-rehabilitation|Chapter 33 Study: Low Vision: Assessment and Rehabilitation":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Normal aging can reduce accommodation, contrast sensitivity and light/dark adaptation while increasing glare sensitivity (pp471–472). Under high contrast, visual acuity in older people without visual impairment shows only a modest decline; low light, lower contrast and glare produce much greater losses (p471, Table 33-1).":["u3"],"copy|Macular disease commonly impairs central vision; glaucoma can cause peripheral field loss, and cataract affects acuity, glare and contrast (p473, Table 33-2).":["u4"],"copy|Assess task performance, not acuity alone: field loss, contrast, glare, visual perception and lighting all matter (p475, p478).":["u5"],"copy|Rehabilitation may help with better-eye acuity below 20/50, field loss despite intact acuity, reduced contrast or adaptation difficulties (p475).":["u6"],"copy|Obtain the person's goals for reading, mobility, medication use, household activities and social participation (p475-476).":["u7"],"copy|Arrange low-vision eye assessment and task-specific device prescription, plus certified rehabilitation professionals for training and environmental assessment (p474-475).":["u8"],"copy|Evaluate acuity and contrast sensitivity; the book recommends the Pelli-Robson chart for practical contrast testing (p475).":["u9"],"copy|Ask about emotional adjustment, depression and family strain; offer counseling or support-group referral when needed (p475).":["u10"],"copy|Functional assessment should occur in relevant settings and include real daily tasks and safety (p477-478).":["u11"],"copy|Match optical, electronic and nonoptical aids to the desired task; demonstration, instruction and practice are part of treatment (p478-481).":["u12"],"copy|Train fixation, scanning and use of remaining vision; a device alone may not restore useful reading (p479-482).":["u13"],"copy|Adjust lighting and control glare individually; use contrast, color, organization and consistent object locations (p482-484).":["u14"],"copy|Teach safe mobility and alternative sensory strategies; involve caregivers in learning the person's abilities and devices (p479, p484-485).":["u15"],"copy|Discuss available rehabilitation funding and services; the chapter describes U.S. rehabilitation, Veterans Health Administration and Medicare routes (p485-486).":["u16"],"copy|In palliative care, keep corrected glasses, call bell and useful devices within reach; identify yourself and explain before touching or moving objects (p486-487).":["u17"],"copy|Preserve autonomy by addressing the person directly and asking what assistance is wanted (p486-487).":["u18"],"why-the-examination-and-the-patient-39-s-experience-can-differ|Why the examination and the patient's experience can differ":["u252"],"copy|The older exam on visual aging asks which change should prompt a search for disease. Reduced accommodation, difficulty distinguishing dark colors and slow adaptation to darkness can accompany aging. A substantial loss of high-contrast acuity calls for evaluation. Healthy aging can still produce a modest decline on that chart (p471–472).":["u253"],"copy|In one sample of 900 older observers, median acuity at age 82 was 20/30 with high contrast. Low contrast yielded 20/55 in bright conditions and 20/120 at low luminance; with glare, low-contrast acuity was 20/160. Ask about signs, steps or faces in the lighting where the person actually struggles (p471).":["u254"],"copy|A central scotoma can impair face recognition and reading. A person may adopt a preferred retinal locus away from the fovea. Training in eccentric viewing and appropriate use of magnification can improve the task even though the scotoma persists (p473, p482).":["u255"],"copy|With peripheral field loss, a person may identify a small object straight ahead yet miss an obstacle to the side. Teach scanning and movement through the person's own environment; ask relatives which conditions trigger hesitation (p473–474, p479, p485).":["u256"],"establish-the-impairment-and-the-task-that-it-interrupts|Establish the impairment and the task that it interrupts":["u257"],"normal-aging-alters-more-than-the-ability-to-resolve-a-letter|Normal aging alters more than the ability to resolve a letter":["u258"],"predict-the-rehabilitation-problem-from-the-disease-pattern|Predict the rehabilitation problem from the disease pattern":["u26"],"turn-a-symptom-questionnaire-into-a-referral-and-a-measurable-goal|Turn a symptom questionnaire into a referral and a measurable goal":["u27"],"match-the-aid-to-the-distance-duration-and-size-of-the-visual-field|Match the aid to the distance, duration and size of the visual field":["u28"],"change-the-environment-so-a-task-stays-possible-when-conditions-change|Change the environment so a task stays possible when conditions change":["u29"],"support-adaptation-access-and-autonomy-at-the-end-of-life|Support adaptation, access and autonomy at the end of life":["u30"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u31"],"copy|Impairment and dependency are different observations. When rating medication identification, check the usual aid, label format and lighting before assigning inability to vision alone (p475–478).":["u32"],"copy|Magnification trades field for detail. A telescope for locating a street sign serves a different purpose from a reverse telescope or prism for detecting hazards (p480–482).":["u33"],"copy|Reading speed, accuracy, comprehension and endurance are separate outcomes; practice can improve one even when the acuity measurement does not change (p472, p482).":["u34"],"copy|A patient who cannot visually recognize the clinician may still understand and choose among treatment options. Introduce yourself and obtain their preferences directly (p486–487).":["u35"],"copy|High-contrast acuity in a well-lit room can conceal disabling loss of contrast sensitivity (p471, p475).":["u36"],"copy|Preserved central acuity does not exclude field loss or remove the indication for rehabilitation (p475).":["u37"],"copy|More illumination may increase glare; test light direction, reflection and the task together (p482–483).":["u38"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u39"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u40"],"copy|CHAPTER 33 / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 33; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Recognize functional vision loss":["u22"],"copy|Assess and refer":["u23"],"copy|Rehabilitation and environment":["u24"],"copy|Low vision has consequences that do not appear on a distance chart.":["u46"],"copy|The person who cannot distinguish one medicine bottle from another may still read large numbers on the wall clock.":["u47"],"copy|The same person may manage well at home, where objects have familiar locations, yet be unable to locate a meal or a call bell in hospital.":["u48"],"copy|The chapter describes rising prevalence of impairment with age across studies, especially after age 75.":["u49"],"copy|Estimates differ according to whether investigators count examination findings or reported functional problems.":["u50"],"copy|In one set of U.S. estimates for 2020, vision loss affected about 4.8% of the population overall and 10% of those aged 50 and older.":["u51"],"copy|These figures describe a defined population and period; neither is an estimate of the proportion who would benefit from a particular device (p469–470).":["u52"],"copy|Consider the indirect consequences when taking the history.":["u53"],"copy|Difficulty recognizing other people can shrink the patient's social world.":["u54"],"copy|Trouble judging a step or reading a food label can appear in the record as a fall or a nutrition problem rather than an eye complaint.":["u55"],"copy|Dual sensory loss makes it harder to rely on spoken instructions in place of print.":["u56"],"copy|Arthritis or weakness can prevent use of a magnifier even when the optical prescription is correct.":["u57"],"copy|The geriatrician's contribution is to identify these interactions and make the rehabilitation referral describe the task, setting and relevant limitations, not merely the eye diagnosis (p469–470, p474–478).":["u58"],"copy|Table 33-1 connects each change to the kind of difficulty a patient reports.":["u59"],"copy|Loss of lens flexibility and ciliary function reduces accommodation, often evident from around age 45 as the need to move near material farther away or use a plus lens.":["u60"],"copy|This is an optical focusing problem.":["u61"],"copy|A high-contrast line on a chart can remain legible while a pale object on a similarly colored surface disappears.":["u62"],"copy|Reduced ocular transmission and smaller pupils make that low-contrast problem worse as illumination falls.":["u63"],"copy|A patient who finds a dark stair edge easily in the examination room may miss it at dusk or when there is no contrast between tread and riser (p470–472).":["u64"],"copy|Increased scattering of light alters visual quality.":["u65"],"copy|A bright window, headlights or a glossy table can obscure a target despite ample total light.":["u66"],"copy|During a transition between outdoor and indoor illumination, slower adaptation can briefly make an otherwise navigable space unsafe.":["u67"],"copy|Ask when hesitancy begins and whether it improves after waiting.":["u68"],"copy|Difficulty discriminating dark colors or pastels also has an immediate practical consequence: two shirts that are distinct in daylight can look identical when selected in a dim cupboard.":["u69"],"copy|The book discusses directed lighting, filters or a brimmed hat and allowing time to adapt.":["u70"],"copy|These address distinct problems; additional overhead light alone may make the glare component worse (p471–472).":["u71"],"copy|The attentional visual field describes how far someone can detect and process rapidly presented information while attention is divided.":["u72"],"copy|It is not identical to the sensory field measured using stationary test lights.":["u73"],"copy|A crowded intersection, signs emerging at the edge of view and simultaneous traffic demands stress that attentional field more than looking for a single light.":["u74"],"copy|Reading also requires attention, coordinated eye movements and finding the next line.":["u75"],"copy|Older adults can retain accuracy while reading more slowly; print size, spacing and contrast can improve the task.":["u76"],"copy|The older exam about a pathologic change uses the contrast between these changes of normal aging and a marked acuity deficit.":["u77"],"copy|The cited age-82 measurements above show exactly why illumination and contrast must be specified before comparing acuities (p471–472).":["u78"],"copy|Use Table 33-2 to distinguish where visual information has been lost.":["u79"],"copy|Macular degeneration removes detail at fixation.":["u80"],"copy|The patient may not describe a black spot, but a face straight ahead can lose its distinguishing features and letters may disappear from a word.":["u81"],"copy|Moving the eyes slightly off the target can place it on functioning retina.":["u82"],"copy|A preferred retinal locus is a substitute fixation site, and training may help the person use it deliberately.":["u83"],"copy|A central scotoma can affect mobility too: poor detail, contrast and depth cues matter when judging a curb, even with peripheral vision remaining (p473, p479–482).":["u84"],"copy|Diabetic retinopathy can produce several patterns in one eye.":["u85"],"copy|Macular edema can blur the foveal image, while hemorrhage, retinal injury or treatment can create scattered central or more peripheral scotomas.":["u86"],"copy|The patient may report fluctuating function rather than a steady decline.":["u87"],"copy|Glaucoma can produce the reverse of the classic macular pattern: a person can recognize a face straight ahead or identify a small object but may miss someone approaching from outside a restricted field.":["u88"],"copy|For cataract, scattered light, glare, altered color, image distortion and loss of contrast may be as disabling as the acuity loss.":["u89"],"copy|Lens extraction with implantation often restores function, while coexisting macular or optic nerve disease can leave a continuing rehabilitation need (p473–475).":["u90"],"copy|Stroke or traumatic brain injury requires a further distinction.":["u91"],"copy|Loss of a hemifield or quadrant removes a region of visual input.":["u92"],"copy|Neglect, agnosia and visuospatial processing difficulty can cause important errors even beyond what the sensory field defect explains.":["u93"],"copy|Ask the person to find items across a tray, locate the sleeve on the affected side or scan a line of text.":["u94"],"copy|A patient can fail at rehabilitation exercises for another impairment because visually presented material or demonstrations are inaccessible.":["u95"],"copy|Identify whether the limiting factor is resolution, field, failure to attend to information or failure to recognize what has been seen; the compensatory strategy differs (p473–475, p478–479).":["u96"],"copy|Table 33-3 contains the VA low-vision functional questionnaire's 20 tasks.":["u97"],"copy|It spans sustained reading, handling money and medicines, navigating familiar places, responding to bright light, finding items on a crowded shelf and managing meals or personal care.":["u98"],"copy|Ask about performance with the aid or technique the person normally uses.":["u99"],"copy|The response for someone who does not perform a task for nonvisual reasons is unscored; treating it as impossible because of vision would misdirect rehabilitation.":["u100"],"copy|A useful goal is concrete enough to repeat later, such as finding the correct medicine and reading its label in the patient's usual kitchen lighting (p475).":["u101"],"copy|Table 33-4 lays out the clinical examination.":["u102"],"copy|Assess distance and near acuity, refraction, internal and external eye health, pressure, central and peripheral fields, color and contrast.":["u103"],"copy|The Pelli-Robson chart tests contrast sensitivity in a way that supplements high-contrast acuity.":["u104"],"copy|Observe the patient with candidate optical, nonoptical or electronic aids.":["u105"],"copy|An ophthalmologist or optometrist with low-vision expertise can prescribe devices, while a rehabilitation professional can teach their use, assess the environment and address independent travel.":["u106"],"copy|Referral may be appropriate with better-eye acuity below 20/50, but can also be appropriate with preserved central acuity and disabling field loss, glare or contrast difficulty (p474–476).":["u107"],"copy|Table 33-5 takes assessment from eye measurements to performance.":["u108"],"copy|Observe fixation, tracking, scanning and use of eye and head movement.":["u109"],"copy|Determine whether the person can separate an object from its background, recognize a critical feature and make sense of a whole scene.":["u110"],"copy|Test near and distant objects under the lighting needed for each task.":["u111"],"copy|Record speed, effort, stress, safety and independence: completing something slowly with a reproducible technique is different from succeeding once by guessing.":["u112"],"copy|The same person may benefit from reading training and from a separate plan for obstacles outside the remaining visual field (p477–479).":["u113"],"copy|Setting changes what can be learned.":["u114"],"copy|In long-term care, ask staff what the person can find in their own room and whether they can still participate in activities when the environment is arranged consistently.":["u115"],"copy|In hospital, identify which needed aids or spectacles remained at home, and choose a time when acute illness allows meaningful participation.":["u116"],"copy|A community agency may offer ready access to rehabilitation specialists and devices; a home visit can reveal glare on a worktop, poor contrast at the bathroom door or an inaccessible position of the reading lamp.":["u117"],"copy|A clinic demonstration of a device remains incomplete until the person has tried a corresponding task (p476–478).":["u118"],"copy|Table 33-6 organizes devices by what they make possible.":["u119"],"copy|A handheld or stand magnifier is well suited to a short task such as checking a price or a recipe.":["u120"],"copy|Spectacle-mounted magnification frees the hands and may be better for sustained reading; it also imposes a working distance that the user must learn to maintain.":["u121"],"copy|A stand, chair with appropriate support or reading surface can help a person who fatigues while holding the head or material at the focal point.":["u122"],"copy|Before prescribing more magnification, ask whether poor hand stability, posture or positioning is what ended the previous attempt (p478–481).":["u123"],"copy|A distant spot task such as finding a street sign has different optical demands.":["u124"],"copy|A handheld monocular or binocular can enlarge the target after the person finds and aligns it.":["u125"],"copy|A spectacle-mounted telescope may be used for television or a spectator activity; selected short-focus devices help at intermediate distances.":["u126"],"copy|A device's presence on the list does not establish driving fitness.":["u127"],"copy|Telescopic magnification narrows the field of view, creating a need for scanning training.":["u128"],"copy|Someone with severe peripheral loss and near-normal central acuity might instead benefit from minification, field-expanding prisms or mirrors to detect an obstacle while walking.":["u129"],"copy|Enlarging a scene can actively worsen that particular task (p479–482).":["u130"],"copy|Electronic magnification allows the person to change size and contrast for reading and writing.":["u131"],"copy|A video system or accessible tablet may provide a larger adjustable view for the same task that was exhausting through a handheld lens.":["u132"],"copy|Speech output, audiobooks or accessible applications allow long passages to be heard when visual reading becomes slow or tiring.":["u133"],"copy|Bold-lined paper, a felt-tip pen and a signature or check-writing guide help position writing.":["u134"],"copy|These are choices according to purpose: a person may want to read a greeting card visually for pleasure, use speech for a long report and use a writing guide to sign a form (p480–482).":["u135"],"copy|Demonstrate and then watch the person use the aid.":["u136"],"copy|For a magnifier, teach focal distance, positioning of the light and how to navigate along and between lines.":["u137"],"copy|For a central scotoma, practice maintaining the preferred retinal locus while moving material through the available field.":["u138"],"copy|Loss of visual span means fewer characters are available at once; comprehension can remain good even when the reading rate is slow.":["u139"],"copy|In the Swedish study cited, 71% of participants could read the newspaper after rehabilitation and 48% could at follow-up three years later, yet the number of fluent readers improved.":["u140"],"copy|Ask about the relevant outcome: the ability to read any newsprint, sustained fluency and reading for pleasure can change differently over time (p479–482).":["u141"],"copy|Table 33-7 groupings prevent a common error: treating every difficulty as a request for larger print.":["u142"],"copy|For poor detail, increase actual size, move the target closer or use magnification.":["u143"],"copy|For restricted peripheral fields with usable acuity, moving back or reducing apparent image size can bring more of the object into view.":["u144"],"copy|Control lighting and observation distance together.":["u145"],"copy|Move a reading lamp to the target rather than flooding the entire room.":["u146"],"copy|Examine the view from the patient's seated or standing position; a light that looks even to the examiner can reflect directly into the person's eyes (p482–483).":["u147"],"copy|Use contrast at the relevant boundary.":["u148"],"copy|Place utensils on a background of a different tone, mark the front edge of a step or select a contrasting switch plate.":["u149"],"copy|Busy patterns can hide a small target despite good overall lighting.":["u150"],"copy|Shadows may help when they reveal the shape of a step but can obscure a curb if they cross it in the wrong direction.":["u151"],"copy|Color helps when it covers a sufficiently large area and contrasts with its background.":["u152"],"copy|Add texture or a raised marker to a dial so identification works by touch as well as sight.":["u153"],"copy|Store similar cans or clothing in a consistent order rather than requiring repeated identification through a lens (p482–484).":["u154"],"copy|Clear predictable pathways are as important as individual markers.":["u155"],"copy|Keep doors fully open or closed and return chairs under the table.":["u156"],"copy|Avoid relocating familiar furniture without telling the person.":["u157"],"copy|Table 33-8 shows how to introduce an unfamiliar room: start at a recognizable point such as the doorway, describe where important surfaces and objects lie, and guide the person around the space and back to the same reference point.":["u158"],"copy|A clock-face or compass description works only if the person finds it intuitive.":["u159"],"copy|An audible machine, familiar smell or bright window can supplement tactile and visual landmarks.":["u160"],"copy|Confirm that the person can independently find the chair, exit, toilet and route back (p482–485).":["u161"],"copy|Changing visual performance can appear inconsistent to family.":["u162"],"copy|A person may pick up a dropped object on a bare floor but fail to recognize a relative's face, or walk confidently at home and hesitate outside a dim restaurant.":["u163"],"copy|Explain how remaining field, contrast and time to adapt account for the difference.":["u164"],"copy|Teaching relatives how to offer help without moving items unpredictably supports confidence and continued device use.":["u165"],"copy|Ask about isolation, fear of falling and withdrawal from activities.":["u166"],"copy|The chapter describes peer support and short-term counseling alongside task practice; mood and adjustment to loss can affect the willingness to try an unfamiliar method (p484–485).":["u167"],"copy|The U.S. services described in the chapter include state rehabilitation agencies, Veterans Health Administration programs and medically prescribed rehabilitation services under Medicare.":["u168"],"copy|Distinguish evaluation and training from purchase of a particular device: eligibility for a therapist visit does not mean every magnifier or electronic aid is funded.":["u169"],"copy|The VA example integrates ophthalmic assessment, orientation and mobility specialists, low-vision therapists, rehabilitation teachers and provision of selected devices.":["u170"],"copy|Confirm local eligibility and payment arrangements when making an actual referral rather than promising the same service in every setting (p485–486).":["u171"],"copy|In palliative care, choose the daily function that matters to the patient today.":["u172"],"copy|Keeping the correct spectacles, magnifier, call bell and water in a predictable location may do more for autonomy than introducing a demanding new device.":["u173"],"copy|Knock, identify yourself, face the person, say who else is present and announce what you are about to do.":["u174"],"copy|Describe where a tray has been placed and ask before leading the person or moving a familiar item.":["u175"],"copy|A patient who cannot identify a staff member's face may still make decisions and understand explanations perfectly well.":["u176"],"copy|Direct the conversation to the person, establish what help is desired and tell them before leaving the room (p486–487).":["u177"],"copy|For a medication-management goal, observe the entire sequence in the usual environment.":["u178"],"copy|Can the person find the bottle on a crowded shelf, distinguish it from a similar container, read the label at a comfortable distance and return it to a consistent place?":["u179"],"copy|If the failure is finding the bottle, rearrangement, a contrasting background or a tactile mark may help.":["u180"],"copy|If the failure is reading the dose, test task lighting, a magnifier or speech output.":["u181"],"copy|Reassess the same sequence with the chosen aid and ask whether the patient can repeat it when tired (p475, p478–480, p482–484).":["u182"],"copy|1.":["u183"],"copy|Which everyday visual changes commonly accompany healthy aging?":["u184"],"copy|2.":["u185"],"copy|What qualification belongs with the older exam answer about acuity loss?":["u186"],"copy|3.":["u187"],"copy|How did lighting change measured acuity in the cited age-82 sample?":["u188"],"copy|4.":["u189"],"copy|What explains slow reading and poor face recognition with macular disease?":["u190"],"copy|5.":["u191"],"copy|Which pattern can diabetic retinopathy produce?":["u192"],"copy|6.":["u193"],"copy|Why may someone with preserved central acuity still miss a doorway or obstacle?":["u194"],"copy|7.":["u195"],"copy|Which chart does the chapter suggest for contrast sensitivity?":["u196"],"copy|8.":["u197"],"copy|Which findings can justify low-vision referral?":["u198"],"copy|9.":["u199"],"copy|What should the first functional interview establish?":["u200"],"copy|10.":["u201"],"copy|What does eccentric viewing train?":["u202"],"copy|11.":["u203"],"copy|Why can fluent comprehension coexist with very slow reading?":["u204"],"copy|12.":["u205"],"copy|How can kitchen controls be easier to locate?":["u206"],"copy|13.":["u207"],"copy|What lighting change can make mobility harder?":["u208"],"copy|14.":["u209"],"copy|What can family members learn from a low-vision evaluation?":["u210"],"copy|15.":["u211"],"copy|How can staff preserve autonomy when entering a room?":["u212"],"copy|Earlier sittings, chapter-level mapping — 2020 Q94.":["u213"],"copy|Answer":["u214","u216","u218","u226","u228","u230","u232","u234","u238","u240","u242","u244","u246","u248","u250"],"copy|Reduced accommodation, poorer dark adaptation, greater glare sensitivity and difficulty discriminating some colors (p471–472).":["u215"],"copy|High-contrast acuity usually declines only modestly in healthy aging; marked loss merits investigation, and low contrast or glare can worsen measured performance substantially (p471).":["u217"],"copy|Median high-contrast acuity was":["u219"],"copy|20/30":["u220"],"copy|, compared with":["u221"],"copy|20/120":["u222"],"copy|under low contrast and low luminance and":["u223"],"copy|20/160":["u224"],"copy|under low contrast with glare (p471).":["u225"],"copy|A central scotoma removes detailed foveal vision and impairs contrast. A preferred retinal locus may develop outside it (p473).":["u227"],"copy|Blurring with macular edema, scattered field loss and fluctuating vision with swelling or hemorrhage (p473).":["u229"],"copy|Peripheral field loss can impair detection away from fixation (p473–475).":["u231"],"copy|The Pelli-Robson chart, which the chapter describes as practical and reliable (p475).":["u233"],"copy|Better-eye acuity below":["u235"],"copy|20/50":["u236"],"copy|, field loss despite intact acuity, reduced contrast sensitivity, glare or light-adaptation difficulty (p475).":["u237"],"copy|Which activities the person wants to recover and in which settings they break down, such as reading medication labels or walking outdoors (p475–478).":["u239"],"copy|Use of a retinal area outside the damaged fovea, including coordination with an appropriate low-vision device (p482).":["u241"],"copy|Central loss reduces visual span and slows word acquisition; identification accuracy and comprehension can remain near normal (p482).":["u243"],"copy|Place high-contrast, tactile marks on frequently used dials and keep items in predictable locations (p484).":["u245"],"copy|Light reflecting from a glossy surface or an abrupt change between bright and dim areas can cause glare or a period of poor adaptation (p472, p482–483).":["u247"],"copy|How light, glare, adaptation and field loss affect performance and which environmental changes or aids actually help (p484–485).":["u249"],"copy|Identify themselves, speak to the person directly, explain before touching or moving objects, and keep glasses and aids within reach in known locations (p486–487).":["u251"]}}
